        "title": "Discrete Maps",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "A discrete map `x_{n+1} = F(x_n)` advances state in integer ticks rather than continuous time. Maps\nare not a poor cousin of flows — they are *more powerful*: a 1D map (logistic) is already chaotic,\nescaping the dimension hierarchy that tames continuous flows (Ch. 00). They arise as **Poincaré\nsections** of flows (Ch. 06), as **numerical integrators** (each step is a map — `numerical-methods/06`),\nand natively in seasonal/digital systems. This chapter develops the map toolkit in full: cobweb\nanalysis, the stability rule `|F'| < 1`, **symbolic dynamics** (the bridge to formal languages), the\n**Hénon** attractor, and the **Smale horseshoe** — the geometric heart of chaos."
    },
    {
